From: Freifunk im Neanderland <projekt@neanderfunk.de>
Subject: MIPS: tlb-r4k: drop the call to r4k_tlb_uniquify()

Reverts the call introduced by stable kernel 5.15.190 with

    35ad7e181541 ("MIPS: mm: tlb-r4k: Uniquify TLB entries on init")

and reworked in 5.15.197 by

    9f048fa48740 ("MIPS: mm: Prevent a TLB shutdown on initial uniquification")

restoring the behaviour of 5.15.189 and everything before it.

Why: on MIPS 74Kc the kernel stops dead in tlb_init() on a cold start. Serial
console, two different SoCs, identical spot:

  TP-Link TL-WR1043ND v2   QCA9558  "Scorpion"
  TP-Link Archer C25 v1    QCA956X  "Dragonfly"

  [    0.000000] Dentry cache hash table entries: 8192 ...
  [    0.000000] Inode-cache hash table entries: 4096 ...
  <nothing further>

The call chain at that point leaves no room for doubt:

  init/main.c:1008   vfs_caches_init_early()   last output
  init/main.c:1009   sort_main_extable()
  init/main.c:1010   trap_init() -> traps.c:2309 tlb_init()
                       -> r4k_tlb_configure() -> r4k_tlb_uniquify()
  init/main.c:1011   mm_init()                 would print "Memory: ..."

Why only on a cold start: r4k_tlb_uniquify() works on whatever the bootloader
left behind in the TLB. On power-up that is arbitrary; after a warm reset it is
the already-uniquified content of the previous run. From the commit message of
9f048fa48740:

  "Depending on the particular CPU implementation a TLB shutdown may occur if
   multiple matching entries are detected upon the execution of a TLBP or the
   TLBWI/TLBWR instructions.  Given that we don't know what entries we have
   been handed ..."

That is exactly why these devices survive any number of sysupgrades and
reboots and never come back after the first power cut.

The 5.15.197 fix is present in 5.15.198 and is not sufficient on 74Kc; nothing
further landed upstream up to 5.15.203. 35ad7e181541 addresses a boot failure
on microAptiv/M5150 cores (7 in 1000 boots there). We do not deploy those
cores, and for our hardware the previous behaviour was correct for a decade.

Placing this under target/linux/generic/ rather than target/linux/ath79/ is
deliberate. arch/mips/mm/tlb-r4k.c is not specific to 74Kc or to ath79; it is
compiled for every r4k-class MIPS core. Our own fleet outage of March 2026
makes the point: of the 57 nodes that went offline, every single one was MIPS,
and they were spread over three OpenWrt targets.

  count  model                       target  SoC        core
     25  Archer C7 v5/v4, A7 v5,     ath79   qca9563    74Kc
         C6 v2, UniFi AC LR/Pro/Mesh
     20  TL-WR1043ND v2/v3,          ath79   qca9558    74Kc
         Archer C7 v2, C5 v1
      5  CPE210 v1, TL-WDR4300 v1,   ath79   ar9344     74Kc
         TL-WDR3500 v1, TL-WDR3600 v1
      4  UniFi AP                    ath79   ar7241     24Kc
      2  AVM FRITZ!Box 7362 SL       lantiq  vr9        34Kc
      1  Archer C50 v3               ramips  mt7628an   24KEc

Not one aarch64 or x86 node was among them, although the fleet contains those
and they kept running throughout. The FRITZ!Box 7362 SL (lantiq/vr9, 34Kc) and
the Archer C50 v3 (ramips/mt7628an, 24KEc) are the reason for the generic
placement in particular: an ath79-only patch would have left them exposed.
ramips-mt7621 (1004Kc) runs the same code as well and is not proven safe.

The core families above are taken from the SoC device trees, not from memory:
ar934x.dtsi, qca955x.dtsi and qca956x.dtsi declare "mips,mips74Kc", ar724x.dtsi
"mips,mips24Kc", vr9.dtsi "mips,mips34Kc" and mt7628an.dtsi "mips,mips24KEc".
Cross-checked against a live TL-WDR3600 v1, which reports MIPS 74Kc V4.12.

Verified on hardware, 2026-09-08, TP-Link Archer C25 v1 on a serial console.
The patched image was loaded into RAM over TFTP and started with "bootm",
without touching the flash. Same spot as before, now with a continuation:

  [    0.000000] Inode-cache hash table entries: 4096 ...
  [    0.000000] Writing ErrCtl register=00000000
  [    0.000000] Memory: 55912K/65536K available (5714K kernel code, ...)

The node boots all the way through to

  [   57.369944] batman_adv: bat0: Interface activated: mesh0

running the patched kernel from RAM against the unchanged rootfs of the old
firmware in flash - same kernel version, same configuration, only tlb-r4k.c
differs. That difference alone decides between "hangs on cold start" and
"runs".

A running node can be checked without a serial console: with the call gone the
function is unused, the compiler drops it, and the symbol disappears.

  grep -q r4k_tlb_uniquify /proc/kallsyms && echo unprotected || echo "fix present"

Confirmed against four unpatched nodes (which show the symbol) and the patched
one (which does not).

Re-evaluate when moving to Gluon 2025.1, which carries a newer kernel.

--- a/arch/mips/mm/tlb-r4k.c
+++ b/arch/mips/mm/tlb-r4k.c
@@ -512,7 +512,7 @@ static int r4k_vpn_cmp(const void *a, co
  * Initialise all TLB entries with unique values that do not clash with
  * what we have been handed over and what we'll be using ourselves.
  */
-static void r4k_tlb_uniquify(void)
+static void __maybe_unused r4k_tlb_uniquify(void)
 {
 	unsigned long tlb_vpns[1 << MIPS_CONF1_TLBS_SIZE];
 	int tlbsize = current_cpu_data.tlbsize;
@@ -616,7 +616,6 @@ static void r4k_tlb_configure(void)
 	temp_tlb_entry = current_cpu_data.tlbsize - 1;
 
 	/* From this point on the ARC firmware is dead.	 */
-	r4k_tlb_uniquify();
 	local_flush_tlb_all();
 
 	/* Did I tell you that ARC SUCKS?  */
